 ## Overview
-Empty Space Filtering is a training-time optimization strategy that accelerates neural volume rendering by restricting computation to the scene's actual content. It solves the problem of wasted computation in empty space by identifying a tight bounding box and filtering out empty samples during ray traversal [S1, S2]. Unlike static acceleration structures that rely on pre-computed occupancy grids or distance maps, this method derives its acceleration structure dynamically from the learned alpha values of a coarse model during the initial training stages [S1, S2]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Distance Map Acceleration](972-distance-map-acceleration.md) by using learned opacity rather than geometric distance, and from [Two-stage Ray Marching with Empty-Space Skipping](2985-two-stage-ray-marching-with-empty-space-skipping.md) by integrating the bounding box shrinkage directly into the training loop of dynamic scenes [S1].
